  "teaching": {
    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M8 V06: Stellar mass and element production. How does stellar mass change the elements a star can make? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "Start when the core's hydrogen runs out. Fusion in the core stops, so the core's pressure drops, and gravity squeezes it smaller. Squeezing turns gravitational potential energy into heat, so the core gets hotter. Hydrogen keeps fusing in a shell around the core, and the extra energy pushes the outer layers out. They swell and cool, and the star becomes a red giant, up and to the right on the HR diagram. When the core reaches about a hundred million kelvin, helium can fuse. Helium nuclei have two protons each, so they repel more strongly than protons, and that's why it takes a much hotter core. Three helium four nuclei join to make carbon twelve. It's called the triple alpha process, because a helium four nucleus is also an alpha particle. Some carbon then captures another helium nucleus, making oxygen sixteen. So the core fills with carbon and oxygen. There's a pattern. Each time a fuel runs out, the core contracts and heats, until the next fuel can burn. Helium and carbon have bigger charges than hydrogen, so they need hotter cores, and each later stage needs a hotter core again. How hot can a core get? That depends on mass. More mass means stronger gravity, which squeezes the core harder and makes it hotter. So a star's mass decides how far up the curve it can climb.",
    "conversationalApproach": "When a star's core hydrogen runs out, can it keep climbing the curve? Here's a star like the Sun, and one twenty-five times more massive. Which one can make heavier elements? Make your prediction. The massive one, by far. And the gold in a ring needs something more extreme still. So how does stellar mass change the elements a star can make? Start when the core's hydrogen runs out. Fusion in the core stops, so the core's pressure drops, and gravity squeezes it smaller. Squeezing turns gravitational potential energy into heat, so the core gets hotter. Hydrogen keeps fusing in a shell around the core, and the extra energy pushes the outer layers out. They swell and cool, and the star becomes a red giant, up and to the right on the HR diagram. When the core reaches about a hundred million kelvin, helium can fuse. Helium nuclei have two protons each, so they repel more strongly than protons, and that's why it takes a much hotter core. Three helium four nuclei join to make carbon twelve. It's called the triple alpha process, because a helium four nucleus is also an alpha particle. Some carbon then captures another helium nucleus, making oxygen sixteen. So the core fills with carbon and oxygen.",
    "openingDecision": "opens on v05's final frame (see \"Opening frame\"); the painted Sun and hot-blue star (about 200 and 340 px) and the curve are solid from the first frame. On \"can it keep climbing the curve\" the dashed \"?\" arrows on the binding curve pulse, amber pen arrow tracing them. On \"one twenty-five times more massive\" the hot-blue star plate tag \"25 M☉\"; the Sun's tag \"1 M☉\". On \"prediction\" a 3 s `Countdown` beside chips \"the Sun-like star\", \"the massive star\", \"both the same\". On \"The massive one, by far\" the \"the massive star\" chip ticks; a reach bar grows beside each star, the massive star's much higher. On \"the gold in a ring\" the painted gold ring `y12p-gold-ring-v1.png` (about 380 px, alone on paper, no hand) appears at the right, an amber hand tag \"Au: beyond iron\" past the curve's peak with a red pen ring round the peak. On \"make\" the chip becomes the heading: \"How does stellar mass change the elements a star can make?\"; the binding curve shrinks to a thumbnail at the top right.",
    "understandingCheck": "When a star's core hydrogen runs out, can it keep climbing the curve? Here's a star like the Sun, and one twenty-five times more massive. Which one can make heavier elements? Make your prediction. The massive one, by far. And the gold in a ring needs something more extreme still. So how does stellar mass change the elements a star can make? Your turn. In the triple alpha process, three helium four nuclei fuse to form carbon twelve. Using the atomic masses given, calculate the energy released, in MeV. Pause here to answer. Last one. Explain why a star of twenty solar masses can produce iron in its core, but a star of one solar mass can't. Pause here to answer.",
    "curriculumScope": "# Year 12 Physics M8 V06: Stellar mass and element production. How does stellar mass change the elements a star can make? (design)\n\nStatus: Revision 3 (13 Nov 2026): visual-standard top-up (stage list, question-card pictures, givens strips, five-line notes, simulation engines named, root rules R1 to R4), visuals only. Narration unchanged. Needs a Codex error check before plates and voicing. Previously Revision 2 (7 Nov 2026): brief 192 findings resolved and visual upgrade to maths-visual-standard.md.\n\nNarration 6,361 characters, 18 scenes, about 547 s, counted by script with the build parser rules (total = sum of scene strings; see \"Narration length estimate\"). Opens on v05's final frame.\n\nScope: sixth video of Year 12 Physics Module 8 (From the Universe to the Atom, Physics Stage 6, 2017 syllabus), the last for the inquiry question \"What evidence is there for the origins of the elements?\". Templates as `phys-y12-m8-v01.md`; `math-a12-d10.md` for the calculation layout. No pre-design pack exists; the pack work is done here (2017 wording from the ledger, site values kept, every number recalculated in Python with HSC data-sheet constants and AME2020 masses, published stellar-model values cited, original attempts, asset reuse). Site lessons read in full (6 Nov, read-only, through the brief 168 text extracts, hashes checked against the live files):\n- `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module8/lesson06.sa.html`, SHA256 `cf2a1403e34de2b647bcee7a61bcf57585eba0940b559d61d1e700ab707f9653` (matches the route; contents listed in `phys-y12-m8-v05.md`); sections used here `follow-the-two-mass-tracks`, `trace-where-the-elements-came-from`.\n- `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module8/lesson07.sa.html`, SHA256 `dcc81fe381fc57ca14e7eb9fe34d8d11842f0ebbcc4cfcc71ac9e84f059a107d` (matches the route; contents listed in `phys-y12-m8-v05.md`); section used here `compare-the-s-and-r-processes`.\n- `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module8/lesson10.sa.html`, SHA256 `8e32208b4f8e050c9d81f3562c0ef767e9f9da1fe483780da2d1ae9cc94524dc` (matches the route), read through `work/brief168/module8-lesson10.sa.txt`: orient and predict (SN 1987A neutrinos), follow the core-collapse sequence (photodisintegration, collapse, bounce, neutrino-driven explosion), compare the two progenitors (Type Ia), inside a neutron star, sort the remnants and limits, calculate and argue (\"Analyse 7 marks\" iron core, \"Assess 6 marks\" supernovae and Earth's composition), independent practice, retrieve and reflect.\n\nRoute entry: `phys-y12-m8-v06`, sections as above. Route start: \"Start from the physical case in the learner question; recall only the earlier result needed for it.\" Route stop: \"Compare main and post-main-sequence nucleosynthesis and iron limits, distinguish energy-releasing fusion from heavier-element production. Stop before treating every supernova detail as mandated.\" Next: \"phys-y12-m8-v07: How did cathode rays and oil drops reveal the electron?\" (designed in parallel by another designer; this design's \"Final frame\" is the agreed join).\n\nOwner rules applied: 2017 syllabus quoted exactly; no people, hands, faces or body outlines (the gold ring is shown alone on a plain ground, never worn; the 2017 observers are not drawn); nuclei, the binding curve, onion layers, the periodic table, reaction equations and calculation strips are coded, HSC style; real objects and sky objects are painted plates (the Sun, two star plates, a planetary nebula, a supernova remnant, a gold ring, the Maltese-cross cathode ray tube; all delivered and inspected 7 Nov), scaled uniformly, never stretched; published values cited (stage durations for a 15 M☉ model star from Woosley and Janka 2005, table 1; GW170817 merger 2017, strontium identified 2019); every number recalculated in Python with 1 u = 931.5 MeV/c² (data sheet) and AME2020 masses; a 3 s prediction; \"Pause here to answer.\" with a 10 s countdown; brief feedback with one crossed-out mistake; original attempts; Australian spelling; no em dashes. Recalled, not re-taught: v05 (binding energy, the curve peaking near iron, hydrostatic balance, the pp chain and CNO cycle, nuclear equations with A and Z), v04 (HR regions: giants up and right, white dwarfs bottom left), v01 (Big Bang hydrogen and helium), m7-v16 (E = mc²), Year 11 (beta decay: a neutron becomes a proton and an electron).\n\nSyllabus content (Physics Stage 6 (2017), Module 8; ledger id `P8-IQ1-07`), quoted from the route JSON `syllabusActions` and the ledger:\n\n- `P8-IQ1-07`: \"investigate the types of nucleosynthesis reactions involved in Main Sequence and Post-Main Sequence stars, including but not limited to:\" \"proton–proton chain\", \"CNO (carbon-nitrogen-oxygen) cycle\"\n\nSource: https://www.nsw.gov.au/sites/default/files/noindex/2025-03/physics-stage-6-syllabus-2017.docx (page 62)\n\n**Boundary:** starts on v05's final frame (the binding curve with dashed \"?\" arrows from ⁴He towards ⁵⁶Fe; the Sun and a massive star, \"how far up?\"). Teaches: what happens when core hydrogen runs out (core contraction and heating, shell burning, red giant); helium burning (about 10⁸ K; triple alpha, 3 ⁴He → ¹²C; ¹²C + ⁴He → ¹⁶O); the pattern (each exhausted fuel leads to contraction, heating and the next fuel; each later stage needs a hotter core; mass sets the core temperature); the Sun-like ending (no carbon burning; planetary nebula; carbon-oxygen white dwarf); the massive-star sequence (carbon, neon, oxygen, silicon burning to iron; onion layers; stages that speed up overall, 15 M☉ model values); the iron limit (the iron-group peak of the curve; no energy-releasing fuel; core collapse in under a second to a city-sized ball; supernova); elements beyond iron by neutron capture and beta decay (⁵⁸Fe → ⁵⁹Fe → ⁵⁹Co), the slow process in giants and the rapid process in neutron-star collisions (GW170817); energy-releasing fusion against element-making neutron capture; dispersal and the origin of each element group. Stops before detailed mass thresholds, the Chandrasekhar and neutron-star mass limits, degeneracy pressure, Type Ia mechanisms, photodisintegration and core-bounce detail, pulsars and neutrino energetics (route stop and site lesson 6 \"beyond the syllabus\").\n\n## Decisions\n\n- **Learner question (route wording kept).** \"How does stellar mass change the elements a star can make?\" The answer: a star climbs the binding curve one fuel at a time, each needing a hotter core (2 to 4); mass sets how hot the core can get, so a Sun-like star stops at carbon and oxygen (5) while a massive star reaches iron (6); iron is the peak, so fusion stops releasing energy and the core collapses (7); heavier elements need neutron capture, mostly in extreme events (8, 9), which makes small amounts but releases no useful energy for the star (10).\n- **Hook (v05's handoff; site lesson 6 predict \"Can the Sun ever produce elements heavier than helium?\").** 3 s prediction chips: \"the Sun-like star\", \"the massive star\", \"both the same\". Answer: the massive star. A painted gold ring (alone, no hand) raises \"even heavier\".\n- **After hydrogen (site `follow-the-two-mass-tracks`; lesson 6 \"Analyse 7 marks\" steps 3 and 4).** Fuel out → pressure down → contraction → heating (gravitational potential energy to thermal energy); hydrogen shell burning; envelope expands and cools: red giant (v04's region recalled).\n- **Helium burning (site \"triple-alpha process: 3 ⁴He → ¹²C + γ (7.27 MeV)\"; \"alpha capture\").** About 10⁸ K because helium's charge is 2 (v05's Coulomb argument recalled); \"alpha particle\" named for helium-4 (Year 11); ¹²C + ⁴He → ¹⁶O (site note 17's correction applied: carbon captures helium, it does not \"fuse to oxygen\").\n- **The pattern (lesson 6 \"Analyse 7 marks\" step 5; site \"only a sufficiently massive star reaches the temperatures\"; Revision 2).** Stated as a rule before the two tracks: each later stage needs a hotter core. Charge explains the first steps only (helium +2 and carbon +6 repel more than hydrogen); the design no longer says \"more protons\" causes every later stage, because neon (Z = 10) burns before oxygen (Z = 8) and neon burning begins by photodisintegration (author note i). Why mass sets temperature: stronger gravity squeezes harder. For a Sun-like star the core stops contracting before carbon ignition (electron degeneracy, author note a; not named, route stop).\n- **Sun-like track (site lesson 6 \"For low-mass stars\").** No carbon burning (about 6 × 10⁸ K needed); planetary nebula (name explained: nothing to do with planets) returns helium, carbon and nitrogen; carbon-oxygen white dwarf (v04's bottom-left region). No mass thresholds in narration; \"a star like the Sun\" and \"more than about eight times the Sun's mass\" (site) only.\n- **Massive track (site \"Progressive core burning: H → He → C → O → Ne → Mg → Si\"; site MC answer \"H → He → C → O → Si → Fe\").** Burning stages carbon, neon, oxygen, silicon, with approximate ignition temperatures (author note b); ash shells (onion layers: H, He, C, O/Ne, Si, Fe from outside in); stage durations for a 15 M☉ model star (Woosley and Janka 2005, table 1: hydrogen about 11 million years; helium about 2 million years; carbon about 2000 years; neon about 0.7 years; oxygen about 2.6 years; silicon about 18 days; Revision 2 replaces the unsourced 25 M☉ list). Narration quotes hydrogen and silicon only and says the stages \"speed up overall\" (neon is shorter than oxygen in this model).\n- **Iron limit (lesson 10 \"Analyse 7 marks\"; route \"iron limits\").** Iron-group nuclei at the peak (v05's curve; \"near iron\" as the kit's caution; Revision 2: no learner copy calls iron \"the most tightly bound\"; nickel-62 is slightly higher, author note d of v05); fusing beyond it takes in energy; no fuel can release energy; the core keeps radiating, pressure falls, collapse in under a second to a city-sized ball of neutrons (site \"roughly Earth's size to ~10 km\"; screen radius 10 to 15 km); rebound and neutrinos expel the outer layers: a core-collapse supernova (one sentence; photodisintegration, bounce physics and the neutron-star limit are not taught: route stop). The painted supernova remnant is labelled \"illustrative\" (kit `ph-stellar-outcomes` guidance).\n- **Neutron capture (site lesson 7 `compare-the-s-and-r-processes`; lesson 7 \"Account 6 marks\" steps 2 and 3).** Neutrons have no charge, so no repulsion; a nucleus captures again while it stays stable, and beta decay raises Z by one when an unstable nucleus is reached (Revision 2: the slow-process path is drawn along stability with stable and unstable stops, not a decay after every capture). Worked chain with real nuclides: ⁵⁸Fe + n → ⁵⁹Fe; ⁵⁹Fe → ⁵⁹Co + e⁻ + antineutrino (half-life 44.5 days; author note c), with A and Z tallies (v05's kit).\n- **Slow and rapid processes (site lesson 7).** Slow: in old giant stars (asymptotic giant branch stars, named on screen only), time to decay between captures, strontium, barium, lead. Rapid: many captures before decay, in neutron-star collisions (GW170817, merger observed 2017: kilonova with freshly made heavy elements; strontium identified in its data in 2019; author note d) and possibly some supernovae (screen tag); gold, platinum, uranium. No neutron-capture rates in narration (author note e).\n- **Energy-releasing against element-making (route stop \"distinguish energy-releasing fusion from heavier-element production\").** Fusion up to iron powers the star; neutron capture beyond iron makes small amounts as a by-product and cannot power a star; heavy elements are rare. The site's \"requires an external supply of energy\" is reworded (site note 21).\n- **Origins (site lesson 6 `trace-where-the-elements-came-from`, lesson 10 \"Assess 6 marks\").** Coded periodic table coloured by main source: Big Bang (H, most He, some Li); Sun-like and intermediate stars shedding layers (C, N, the slow process heavy elements); massive stars and supernovae (O to Fe; Type Ia supernovae also make much iron, tag only); neutron-star collisions (rapid process heavy elements). Oxygen is assigned to massive stars, not low-mass stars (site note 22). Dispersal by winds, planetary nebulae and supernovae, then new stars, planets and \"you\" (narration only; no people drawn).\n- **Memory aid (scene 12, new): \"Heavier star, hotter core, heavier elements, until iron says stop. Past iron, add neutrons.\"** See \"Memory aid check\".\n- **Attempts (original, HSC style).** A1 (3 marks): the energy released by the triple alpha process with atomic masses: 0.007809 u, 7.27 MeV. Crossed: subtracting the wrong way and concluding energy is absorbed. A2 (3 marks, explain): why a 20 M☉ star can make iron in its core and a 1 M☉ star cannot. Crossed: \"the massive star has more fuel, so it burns for longer and gets further\".\n- **Significant figures.** Masses as given (six decimal places); energies to three figures; temperatures and durations to one figure (\"about\").\n- **Handoff (route next, v07 written in parallel).** Every atom these stars make is a nucleus with electrons; electrons were unknown until the 1890s, when glowing glass tubes gave the first clue. \"Next: how did cathode rays and oil drops reveal the electron?\". See \"Final frame\" (exact description for the v07 designer). Revision 2: the tube in the final frame is the delivered Maltese-cross tube `y12p-maltese-cross-tube-v1.png` (root decision 17), not the held `y12p-crt-tube-v1.png`; v07 Revision 2 opens on it.\n- **Colour key (as v05):** protons rose, neutrons grey, electrons navy, antineutrinos small grey \"ν̄\", helium-4 nuclei two rose and two grey; heavier nuclei larger labelled discs; onion layers in graded warm tones with labels; gravity arrows navy, pressure arrows amber; periodic table cells coloured by source (Big Bang pale blue, Sun-like stars green, massive stars and supernovae amber, neutron-star collisions purple), with a legend so colour is never the only cue.\n- **Colour key (Revision 2, fixed for every scene).** As above. Core temperature red (the ladder, the readouts and the slider); stellar mass purple (the mass slider, the \"1 M☉\" and \"25 M☉\" tags and the reach bars); energy amber (climbing arrows, \"releases energy\"); the iron barrier and \"takes in energy\" red; stable nuclei on the N-Z grid ink rings, unstable nuclei red-outlined rings; framed answers in a dark-ink box; crossed chips and their hand tags red. Hand-font tags take the colour of the quantity they mark.\n- **Type sizes (Revision 2, 1080p).** Current working line at least 44 px (reaction equations, scene 8 chain and feedback rows 52 px; framed answers 56 px); live readouts (the mass slider's mass, hottest core, last fuel and end state; the core temperature; the stage clock) about 52 px; ladder marks, onion labels, legend and chips 30 to 36 px; hand-font tags at least 30 px; question text 36 px; key notes 34 px; nothing a student must read below 26 px. All text above the bottom 12% of the frame.\n- **Anchor (Revision 2).** v05's final frame with the painted Sun and hot-blue star is the anchor; the painted gold ring `y12p-gold-ring-v1.png` (delivered, 1402 × 1122, RGBA, inspected 7 Nov: a plain polished band, alone) joins it in scene 1 and returns in 8 and 10 when gold's origin is answered. The painted planetary nebula (5, 10) and supernova remnant (7, 10), the cool-red star (2) and the Maltese-cross tube (17) carry the other stages (all delivered, inspected).\n- **Simulation (Revision 2).** The core live control is the mass slider in scene 4: a purple slider beside the core temperature ladder sets the star's mass, and live readouts show \"mass\", \"hottest core\", \"last fuel\" and \"ends as\" while the reach bar rises (1 M☉ and 4 M☉: about 10⁸ K, helium, a carbon-oxygen white dwarf; more than about 8 M☉, shown at 15 and 25 M☉: about 3 × 10⁹ K, silicon, an iron core and a supernova; ignition temperatures from author note b; no remnant mass limits). The stage clock in 6 (15 M☉ model durations) and the N-Z capture staircase in 8 and 9 are the other live elements.\n- **Board stages (Revision 2).** Every scene has its own stage: the curve with the Sun, star and ring (1), the swelling star (2), inside the core (3), the live ladder and mass slider (4), the Sun-like track with the nebula (5), the onion with the stage clock (6), the curve and collapse with the remnant (7), the N-Z grid (8, 9, about 70 s), the two-column panel (10), the periodic table (11). No set-up runs past about 70 s.\n- **Question cards (Revision 2).** Each card arrives with its full text and a picture in one move (A1: three coded ⁴He discs meeting and one ¹²C disc, no masses on the picture; A2: two painted stars, the Sun \"1 M☉\" and the hot-blue star \"20 M☉\", each with an empty core circle). On the answer side the card shrinks to the top, a givens strip lists the data (A1: \"⁴He 4.002603 u; ¹²C 12.000000 u; 1 u = 931.5 MeV/c²\"; A2: \"20 M☉ star; 1 M☉ star; iron in the core?\"), results are framed and one classic slip is crossed.\n- **Site sources used:** lesson 6: predict (Sun's fate; heavier than helium?) (1); `follow-the-two-mass-tracks` (core contracts and heats; shell burning; red giant; triple alpha at 10⁸ K, 7.27 MeV; planetary nebula; white dwarf; progressive burning to an iron core; iron absorbs energy; collapse; supernova) (2 to 7); `trace-where-the-elements-came-from` (element sources; r-process; GW170817) (9, 11); misconceptions (supernova is not overheating; the Sun will not become a neutron star) (5, 7); \"Analyse 7 marks\" (fuel out, pressure down, contraction, hotter core, mass decides) (2, 4). Lesson 7: `compare-the-s-and-r-processes` (capture plus beta decay; s-process in AGB stars; r-process; GW170817 kilonova) (8, 9); \"Account 6 marks\" (constraint, neutrons avoid repulsion, capture then beta decay, sites, dispersal) (8 to 10). Lesson 10: `follow-the-core-collapse-sequence` (collapse in under a second, about 10 km, neutrinos, ejection) (7); \"Analyse 7 marks\" (equilibrium, shells, binding peak, energy balance, unopposed collapse) (6, 7); \"Assess 6 marks\" (production and dispersal; C, N, O made in normal stellar lives) (10, 11). Not used: Chandrasekhar and TOV limits, degeneracy pressure, Type Ia mechanism, neutron-star density and pulsar calculations, photodisintegration and neutrino energetics (route stop; site extension).\n- **Site notes (quoted from the site, read 6 Nov; for root to log in `../site-corrections-phys-y12.md` if accepted; continuing after v05's note 20; v05's note 17 on \"carbon may fuse to oxygen\" also applies here):**\n  21. Energy of neutron capture (lesson 7 \"Account 6 marks\" step 2): \"Forming them requires an external supply of energy and an environment rich in free neutrons\". Neutron capture itself releases energy (several MeV per capture); what is scarce is a large supply of free neutrons, and building nuclei beyond the iron peak gives no net energy to support a star. Suggest \"requires an environment rich in free neutrons, and releases no net energy that could support a star\".\n  22. Oxygen's origin (lesson 6 `trace-where-the-elements-came-from`; lesson 7 HSC tip): \"Carbon, oxygen, nitrogen: Helium and carbon burning in low- and intermediate-mass stars\" and \"C, O from low-mass stellar cores\". Low- and intermediate-mass stars lock most of their oxygen in white dwarfs; most of the oxygen in the Universe was released by massive stars in core-collapse supernovae. Carbon and nitrogen are largely from low- and intermediate-mass stars. Suggest moving oxygen to \"massive stars\".\n  23. Timing (lesson 6 `trace-where-the-elements-came-from`): \"Hydrogen and most helium: Big Bang nucleosynthesis (first 3 minutes)\"; see v01's site note 1 (fusion ran to about 20 minutes).\n  24. Mass limits presented as fixed (lesson 6 HSC tip \"Memorise the mass thresholds\" and lesson 10 \"neutron star (if < 3 M☉)\"): the site's own depth notes call these extension and model dependent, and the maximum neutron-star mass is now estimated at about 2.2 to 2.3 M☉. Suggest \"about 2 to 3 M☉ (model dependent)\" and removing \"memorise\".\n  25. Checked and correct: triple alpha 7.27 MeV; 10⁸ K helium ignition; silicon burning at about 3 × 10⁹ K to iron-group nuclei; collapse in under a second; 99% of energy as neutrinos; GW170817 as an r-process site; Fe-56 peak for HSC with the Ni-62 note.\n- **Cause chain taught, in order:** (1) when core hydrogen runs out, pressure falls and the core contracts and heats, while the envelope swells (red giant) (2); (2) at about 10⁸ K helium fuses to carbon and oxygen (3); (3) each later fuel needs a hotter core (helium and carbon because their bigger charges repel more), and only a more massive star's gravity can squeeze its core that hot (4); (4) so a Sun-like star stops at carbon and oxygen and leaves a white dwarf (5), while a massive star climbs to iron in ever shorter stages (6); (5) iron is the peak, so no fusion can release energy, the core collapses and the star explodes (7); (6) beyond iron, neutrons (uncharged) are captured and beta decay raises the element (8), slowly in giants, rapidly in neutron-star collisions (9); (7) that makes heavy elements in small amounts without powering the star, and the dying stars spread everything into space (10, 11).\n- **Entry knowledge (route `entryKnowledge`):** v05 (binding curve, hydrostatic balance, nuclear equations); v04 (HR regions); v01 (Big Bang elements); Year 11 (beta decay, nuclear notation).\n- **Learner-visible vocabulary (plain meaning at first use):** shell burning (fusion in a layer around the core, 2); red giant (recalled, 2); alpha particle (a helium-4 nucleus, 3); triple alpha process (3); planetary nebula (a glowing shell of gas a dying Sun-like star throws off; nothing to do with planets, 5); white dwarf (recalled, 5); supernova (the explosion of a massive star after its core collapses, 7); neutron capture (a nucleus absorbing a free neutron, 8); beta decay (recalled: a neutron turning into a proton and giving out an electron, 8); slow process, rapid process (9).\n- **Legacy logic ported:** none. Explainer families reused: `m8-stellar-evolution` (src/explainer/physics/module8: \"Distinguish a Sun-like path from a massive-star core-collapse path\"), within its limits (\"No universal remnant mass boundary\"; \"No Type Ia mechanism\"); `m8-nucleosynthesis` (binding and element origins), within its limits (\"Iron-group maximum\"; \"Neutron capture is not shown step by step; no claim all supernovae provide the main r-process\"): this design adds the step-by-step capture chain with real nuclides and keeps \"possibly some supernovae\" as a tag.\n- **New explainer motion:** **core contraction and envelope swell** (a core shrinking and brightening while the outer layer balloons and reddens, linked to an HR thumbnail point moving up and right); **triple alpha** (three ⁴He discs merging into ¹²C, then ¹²C + ⁴He → ¹⁶O); **core temperature ladder** (a vertical scale with ignition marks for H, He, C, Ne, O, Si and two \"reach\" bars, one stopping below carbon for the Sun-like star, one reaching silicon for the massive star); **onion layers** (concentric shells filling from the centre as each stage completes, with a stage-duration clock that speeds up); **binding-curve stop** (v05's curve with a red barrier at the peak and an arrow bending downwards beyond it, \"takes in energy\"); **core collapse** (a city-scale bracket as the iron core shrinks in a fraction of a second, outer layers rebounding outwards); **neutron capture chain** (a nuclide strip on an N-Z grid: capture moves right, beta decay moves diagonally up-left, with A and Z tallies); **periodic table by source** (cells fill colour by source, with legend); `AlignedAlgebra`; `NumberTicker`; `Countdown` (3 s in scene 1; 10 s in scenes 13 and 15); `QuestionCard`; `Chip` with `c