    "visualSystem": "# Year 12 Physics M8 V06: Stellar mass and element production. How does stellar mass change the elements a star can make? (design)\n\nStatus: Revision 3 (13 Nov 2026): visual-standard top-up (stage list, question-card pictures, givens strips, five-line notes, simulation engines named, root rules R1 to R4), visuals only. Narration unchanged. Needs a Codex error check before plates and voicing. Previously Revision 2 (7 Nov 2026): brief 192 findings resolved and visual upgrade to maths-visual-standard.md.\n\nNarration 6,361 characters, 18 scenes, about 547 s, counted by script with the build parser rules (total = sum of scene strings; see \"Narration length estimate\"). Opens on v05's final frame.\n\nScope: sixth video of Year 12 Physics Module 8 (From the Universe to the Atom, Physics Stage 6, 2017 syllabus), the last for the inquiry question \"What evidence is there for the origins of the elements?\". Templates as `phys-y12-m8-v01.md`; `math-a12-d10.md` for the calculation layout. No pre-design pack exists; the pack work is done here (2017 wording from the ledger, site values kept, every number recalculated in Python with HSC data-sheet constants and AME2020 masses, published stellar-model values cited, original attempts, asset reuse). Site lessons read in full (6 Nov, read-only, through the brief 168 text extracts, hashes checked against the live files):\n- `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module8/lesson06.sa.html`, SHA256 `cf2a1403e34de2b647bcee7a61bcf57585eba0940b559d61d1e700ab707f9653` (matches the route; contents listed in `phys-y12-m8-v05.md`); sections used here `follow-the-two-mass-tracks`, `trace-where-the-elements-came-from`.\n- `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module8/lesson07.sa.html`, SHA256 `dcc81fe381fc57ca14e7eb9fe34d8d11842f0ebbcc4cfcc71ac9e84f059a107d` (matches the route; contents listed in `phys-y12-m8-v05.md`); section used here `compare-the-s-and-r-processes`.\n- `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module8/lesson10.sa.html`, SHA256 `8e32208b4f8e050c9d81f3562c0ef767e9f9da1fe483780da2d1ae9cc94524dc` (matches the route), read through `work/brief168/module8-lesson10.sa.txt`: orient and predict (SN 1987A neutrinos), follow the core-collapse sequence (photodisintegration, collapse, bounce, neutrino-driven explosion), compare the two progenitors (Type Ia), inside a neutron star, sort the remnants and limits, calculate and argue (\"Analyse 7 marks\" iron core, \"Assess 6 marks\" supernovae and Earth's composition), independent practice, retrieve and reflect.\n\nRoute entry: `phys-y12-m8-v06`, sections as above. Route start: \"Start from the physical case in the learner question; recall only the earlier result needed for it.\" Route stop: \"Compare main and post-main-sequence nucleosynthesis and iron limits, distinguish energy-releasing fusion from heavier-element production. Stop before treating every supernova detail as mandated.\" Next: \"phys-y12-m8-v07: How did cathode rays and oil drops reveal the electron?\" (designed in parallel by another designer; this design's \"Final frame\" is the agreed join).\n\nOwner rules applied: 2017 syllabus quoted exactly; no people, hands, faces or body outlines (the gold ring is shown alone on a plain ground, never worn; the 2017 observers are not drawn); nuclei, the binding curve, onion layers, the periodic table, reaction equations and calculation strips are coded, HSC style; real objects and sky objects are painted plates (the Sun, two star plates, a planetary nebula, a supernova remnant, a gold ring, the Maltese-cross cathode ray tube; all delivered and inspected 7 Nov), scaled uniformly, never stretched; published values cited (stage durations for a 15 M☉ model star from Woosley and Janka 2005, table 1; GW170817 merger 2017, strontium identified 2019); every number recalculated in Python with 1 u = 931.5 MeV/c² (data sheet) and AME2020 masses; a 3 s prediction; \"Pause here to answer.\" with a 10 s countdown; brief feedback with one crossed-out mistake; original attempts; Australian spelling; no em dashes. Recalled, not re-taught: v05 (binding energy, the curve peaking near iron, hydrostatic balance, the pp chain and CNO cycle, nuclear equations with A and Z), v04 (HR regions: giants up and right, white dwarfs bottom left), v01 (Big Bang hydrogen and helium), m7-v16 (E = mc²), Year 11 (beta decay: a neutron becomes a proton and an electron).\n\nSyllabus content (Physics Stage 6 (2017), Module 8; ledger id `P8-IQ1-07`), quoted from the route JSON `syllabusActions` and the ledger:\n\n- `P8-IQ1-07`: \"investigate the types of nucleosynthesis reactions involved in Main Sequence and Post-Main Sequence stars, including but not limited to:\" \"proton–proton chain\", \"CNO (carbon-nitrogen-oxygen) cycle\"\n\nSource: https://www.nsw.gov.au/sites/default/files/noindex/2025-03/physics-stage-6-syllabus-2017.docx (page 62)\n\n**Boundary:** starts on v05's final frame (the binding curve with dashed \"?\" arrows from ⁴He towards ⁵⁶Fe; the Sun and a massive star, \"how far up?\"). Teaches: what happens when core hydrogen runs out (core contraction and heating, shell burning, red giant); helium burning (about 10⁸ K; triple alpha, 3 ⁴He → ¹²C; ¹²C + ⁴He → ¹⁶O); the pattern (each exhausted fuel leads to contraction, heating and the next fuel; each later stage needs a hotter core; mass sets the core temperature); the Sun-like ending (no carbon burning; planetary nebula; carbon-oxygen white dwarf); the massive-star sequence (carbon, neon, oxygen, silicon burning to iron; onion layers; stages that speed up overall, 15 M☉ model values); the iron limit (the iron-group peak of the curve; no energy-releasing fuel; core collapse in under a second to a city-sized ball; supernova); elements beyond iron by neutron capture and beta decay (⁵⁸Fe → ⁵⁹Fe → ⁵⁹Co), the slow process in giants and the rapid process in neutron-star collisions (GW170817); energy-releasing fusion against element-making neutron capture; dispersal and the origin of each element group. Stops before detailed mass thresholds, the Chandrasekhar and neutron-star mass limits, degeneracy pressure, Type Ia mechanisms, photodisintegration and core-bounce detail, pulsars and neutrino energetics (route stop and site lesson 6 \"beyond the syllabus\").\n\n## Decisions\n\n- **Learner question (route wording kept).** \"How does stellar mass change the elements a star can make?\" The answer: a star climbs the binding curve one fuel at a time, each needing a hotter core (2 to 4); mass sets how hot the core can get, so a Sun-like star stops at carbon and oxygen (5) while a massive star reaches iron (6); iron is the peak, so fusion stops releasing energy and the core collapses (7); heavier elements need neutron capture, mostly in extreme events (8, 9), which makes small amounts but releases no useful energy for the star (10).\n- **Hook (v05's handoff; site lesson 6 predict \"Can the Sun ever produce elements heavier than helium?\").** 3 s prediction chips: \"the Sun-like star\", \"the massive star\", \"both the same\". Answer: the massive star. A painted gold ring (alone, no hand) raises \"even heavier\".\n- **After hydrogen (site `follow-the-two-mass-tracks`; lesson 6 \"Analyse 7 marks\" steps 3 and 4).** Fuel out → pressure down → contraction → heating (gravitational potential energy to thermal energy); hydrogen shell burning; envelope expands and cools: red giant (v04's region recalled).\n- **Helium burning (site \"triple-alpha process: 3 ⁴He → ¹²C + γ (7.27 MeV)\"; \"alpha capture\").** About 10⁸ K because helium's charge is 2 (v05's Coulomb argument recalled); \"alpha particle\" named for helium-4 (Year 11); ¹²C + ⁴He → ¹⁶O (site note 17's correction applied: carbon captures helium, it does not \"fuse to oxygen\").\n- **The pattern (lesson 6 \"Analyse 7 marks\" step 5; site \"only a sufficiently massive star reaches the temperatures\"; Revision 2).** Stated as a rule before the two tracks: each later stage needs a hotter core. Charge explains the first steps only (helium +2 and carbon +6 repel more than hydrogen); the design no longer says \"more protons\" causes every later stage, because neon (Z = 10) burns before oxygen (Z = 8) and neon burning begins by photodisintegration (author note i). Why mass sets temperature: stronger gravity squeezes harder. For a Sun-like star the core stops contracting before carbon ignition (electron degeneracy, author note a; not named, route stop).\n- **Sun-like track (site lesson 6 \"For low-mass stars\").** No carbon burning (about 6 × 10⁸ K needed); planetary nebula (name explained: nothing to do with planets) returns helium, carbon and nitrogen; carbon-oxygen white dwarf (v04's bottom-left region). No mass thresholds in narration; \"a star like the Sun\" and \"more than about eight times the Sun's mass\" (site) only.\n- **Massive track (site \"Progressive core burning: H → He → C → O → Ne → Mg → Si\"; site MC answer \"H → He → C → O → Si → Fe\").** Burning stages carbon, neon, oxygen, silicon, with approximate ignition temperatures (author note b); ash shells (onion layers: H, He, C, O/Ne, Si, Fe from outside in); stage durations for a 15 M☉ model star (Woosley and Janka 2005, table 1: hydrogen about 11 million years; helium about 2 million years; carbon about 2000 years; neon about 0.7 years; oxygen about 2.6 years; silicon about 18 days; Revision 2 replaces the unsourced 25 M☉ list). Narration quotes hydrogen and silicon only and says the stages \"speed up overall\" (neon is shorter than oxygen in this model).\n- **Iron limit (lesson 10 \"Analyse 7 marks\"; route \"iron limits\").** Iron-group nuclei at the peak (v05's curve; \"near iron\" as the kit's caution; Revision 2: no learner copy calls iron \"the most tightly bound\"; nickel-62 is slightly higher, author note d of v05); fusing beyond it takes in energy; no fuel can release energy; the core keeps radiating, pressure falls, collapse in under a second to a city-sized ball of neutrons (site \"roughly Earth's size to ~10 km\"; screen radius 10 to 15 km); rebound and neutrinos expel the outer layers: a core-collapse supernova (one sentence; photodisintegration, bounce physics and the neutron-star limit are not taught: route stop). The painted supernova remnant is labelled \"illustrative\" (kit `ph-stellar-outcomes` guidance).\n- **Neutron capture (site lesson 7 `compare-the-s-and-r-processes`; lesson 7 \"Account 6 marks\" steps 2 and 3).** Neutrons have no charge, so no repulsion; a nucleus captures again while it stays stable, and beta decay raises Z by one when an unstable nucleus is reached (Revision 2: the slow-process path is drawn along stability with stable and unstable stops, not a decay after every capture). Worked chain with real nuclides: ⁵⁸Fe + n → ⁵⁹Fe; ⁵⁹Fe → ⁵⁹Co + e⁻ + antineutrino (half-life 44.5 days; author note c), with A and Z tallies (v05's kit).\n- **Slow and rapid processes (site lesson 7).** Slow: in old giant stars (asymptotic giant branch stars, named on screen only), time to decay between captures, strontium, barium, lead. Rapid: many captures before decay, in neutron-star collisions (GW170817, merger observed 2017: kilonova with freshly made heavy elements; strontium identified in its data in 2019; author note d) and possibly some supernovae (screen tag); gold, platinum, uranium. No neutron-capture rates in narration (author note e).\n- **Energy-releasing against element-making (route stop \"distinguish energy-releasing fusion from heavier-element production\").** Fusion up to iron powers the star; neutron capture beyond iron makes small amounts as a by-product and cannot power a star; heavy elements are rare. The site's \"requires an external supply of energy\" is reworded (site note 21).\n- **Origins (site lesson 6 `trace-where-the-elements-came-from`, lesson 10 \"Assess 6 marks\").** Coded periodic table coloured by main source: Big Bang (H, most He, some Li); Sun-like and intermediate stars shedding layers (C, N, the slow process heavy elements); massive stars and supernovae (O to Fe; Type Ia supernovae also make much iron, tag only); neutron-star collisions (rapid process heavy elements). Oxygen is assigned to massive stars, not low-mass stars (site note 22). Dispersal by winds, planetary nebulae and supernovae, then new stars, planets and \"you\" (narration only; no people drawn).\n- **Memory aid (scene 12, new): \"Heavier star, hotter core, heavier elements, until iron says stop. Past iron, add neutrons.\"** See \"Memory aid check\".\n- **Attempts (original, HSC style).** A1 (3 marks): the energy released by the triple alpha process with atomic masses: 0.007809 u, 7.27 MeV. Crossed: subtracting the wrong way and concluding energy is absorbed. A2 (3 marks, explain): why a 20 M☉ star can make iron in its core and a 1 M☉ star cannot. Crossed: \"the massive star has more fuel, so it burns for longer and gets further\".\n- **Significant figures.** Masses as given (six decimal places); energies to three figures; temperatures and durations to one figure (\"about\").\n- **Handoff (route next, v07 written in parallel).** Every atom these stars make is a nucleus with electrons; electrons were unknown until the 1890s, when glowing glass tubes gave the first clue. \"Next: how did cathode rays and oil drops reveal the electron?\". See \"Final frame\" (exact description for the v07 designer). Revision 2: the tube in the final frame is the delivered Maltese-cross tube `y12p-maltese-cross-tube-v1.png` (root decision 17), not the held `y12p-crt-tube-v1.png`; v07 Revision 2 opens on it.\n- **Colour key (as v05):** protons rose, neutrons grey, electrons navy, antineutrinos small grey \"ν̄\", helium-4 nuclei two rose and two grey; heavier nuclei larger labelled discs; onion layers in graded warm tones with labels; gravity arrows navy, pressure arrows amber; periodic table cells coloured by source (Big Bang pale blue, Sun-like stars green, massive stars and supernovae amber,